var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#PackageScanner.jl","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"(Image: Build Status)\n\nA comprehensive Julia package for scanning and analyzing research replication packages. PackageScanner combines PII detection, file path analysis, metadata extraction, and package validation in one tool.\n\nThis package is part of the JPE Data Editor's toolkit. Included functionality has been greatly inspired by the AEA Data Editor's replication template setup.","title":"PackageScanner.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#PII-Detection","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"Based on the methodology from J-PAL's PII-Scan, scans variable names, labels, and code for personally identifiable information (PII) to help researchers comply with privacy regulations like GDPR. See below for legal implications.\n\nFast Stata and CSV readers: Reads only the first 1000 rows to avoid performance issues\nMulti-format data support: Uses R's rio package to read SPSS, SAS, Excel, CSV, and many other formats\nSmart detection: Minimizes false positives (e.g., names(x) vs first_name)\nCustomizable: Add your own PII search terms","title":"PII Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"#Package-Analysis","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"Comprehensive tools for analyzing research replication packages:\n\nFile classification: Automatically categorizes files as code, data, or documentation\nPath detection: Identifies Windows/Unix file paths and hardcoded values in code\nFile metadata: Analyzes sizes, checksums, and detects duplicates\nREADME analysis: Parses README files (MD/PDF) for key information\nCode statistics: Integration with cloc for line counting\nReport generation: Creates markdown reports for all analyses","title":"Package Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/floswald/PackageScanner.jl\")\n\nRequirements:\n\nJulia 1.10+\nR with the rio and haven packages installed\n\n# In R:\ninstall.packages(c(\"rio\", \"haven\"))\n\nOptional: cloc for code statistics (brew install cloc on macOS)","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-Start","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Full-Package-Analysis","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"using PackageScanner\n\n# Run comprehensive analysis on a replication package\nPackageScanner.precheck_package(\"/path/to/replication-package\")\n\nThis single command performs:\n\nFile classification (code/data/docs)\nFile metadata analysis (sizes, checksums, duplicates)\nCode line counting (via cloc if available)\nFile path detection in code\nPII detection in data and code\nREADME analysis\n\nAll reports are written to generated/ folder at the package root.","title":"Full Package Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"#PII-Scanning-Only","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"using PackageScanner\n\n# Define your files\ndata_files = [\"data/survey.dta\", \"data/admin.csv\"]\ncode_files = [\"src/clean.R\", \"src/analysis.py\"]\n\n# Scan for PII\ndata_results = PackageScanner.scan_data_files(data_files)\ncode_results = PackageScanner.scan_code_files(code_files)\n\n# Generate report\nPackageScanner.write_pii_report(data_results, code_results, \"output/\")","title":"PII Scanning Only"},{"category":"section","location":"#Detailed-Usage","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"","title":"Detailed Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"#PII-Detection-2","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"# Scan a single data file\nmatches = PackageScanner.scan_data_file(\"data/survey.dta\")\n\n# Scan with strict matching (word boundaries only)\nmatches = PackageScanner.scan_data_file(\"data/survey.dta\", strict=true)\n\n# Add custom PII terms\nmatches = PackageScanner.scan_data_file(\"data/survey.dta\", custom_terms=[\"patient_id\", \"taxpayer\"])\n\n# Scan code for PII references\ncode_matches = PackageScanner.scan_code_file(\"src/analysis.R\")","title":"PII Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"#File-Classification","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"# Classify files by type\ncode_files = PackageScanner.classify_files(\"/path/to/package\", \"code\", \"output/\")\ndata_files = PackageScanner.classify_files(\"/path/to/package\", \"data\", \"output/\")\ndocs_files = PackageScanner.classify_files(\"/path/to/package\", \"docs\", \"output/\")","title":"File Classification"},{"category":"section","location":"#File-Metadata-Analysis","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"# Analyze file sizes and detect duplicates\nmetadata = PackageScanner.generate_file_sizes_md5(\"/path/to/package\", \"output/\")","title":"File Metadata Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"#Path-Detection","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"# Check code files for problematic file paths\nPackageScanner.file_paths(code_files, \"output/\")","title":"Path Detection"},{"category":"section","location":"#Default-PII-Terms","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"The package searches for these terms by default (based on J-PAL PII-Scan):\n\naddress, bday, beneficiary, birth, birthday, city, dob, email,  firstname, fname, lastname, lname, name, phone, ssn, and more...\n\nSee PackageScanner.DEFAULT_PII_TERMS for the complete list.","title":"Default PII Terms"},{"category":"section","location":"#Example-Reports","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"PackageScanner generates multiple markdown reports:\n\nreport-pii.md - PII detection summary\nreport-file-paths.md - File path analysis\nreport-file-sizes.md - File metadata\nreport-duplicates.md - Duplicate files\nreport-readme.md - README analysis\nreport-cloc.md - Code statistics\nAnd more...","title":"Example Reports"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contributing","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"Contributions welcome! Please open an issue or PR.","title":"Contributing"},{"category":"section","location":"#License","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"MIT License - see LICENSE file","title":"License"},{"category":"section","location":"#Acknowledgments","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"PII detection based on J-PAL PII-Scan\nUses R's rio package for multi-format data support\nIntegrates JPEtools.jl functionality for comprehensive package analysis","title":"Acknowledgments"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-does-GDPR-Mean-for-Researchers?","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"There may be other legal provisions for publishing your research data beyond GDPR\n\nHere are three main things to keep in mind:\n\nRight to be Forgotten (Article 17): Researchers must be able to identify and delete an individual's data upon request. PII in datasets makes this possible - without proper tracking of identifiable information, you cannot comply with deletion requests. Conversely, inadvertently retaining PII when it should have been anonymized creates compliance risk.\nData Minimization Principle (Article 5): You should only collect and retain personal data that is adequate, relevant, and limited to what's necessary for your research purpose. A PII scanner helps identify when you're holding more identifiable information than needed - for instance, keeping full names and addresses when only age ranges and region codes would suffice for analysis.\nBreach Notification Requirements (Article 33-34): If you suffer a data breach involving personal data, you must notify authorities within 72 hours and inform affected individuals. A PII scanner helps you quickly assess what was exposed in a breach - whether it's anonymized research data (lower risk) or datasets containing names, SSNs, or other direct identifiers (high risk requiring immediate notification).\n\nBackground:\n\nFull Legal Text - EU GDPR","title":"What does GDPR Mean for Researchers?"},{"category":"section","location":"#All-Docstrings","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"","title":"All Docstrings"},{"category":"constant","location":"#PackageScanner.DEFAULT_PII_TERMS","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"DEFAULT_PII_TERMS\n\nDefault list of PII search terms based on J-PAL PII-Scan. These terms are commonly found in variable names that contain personally identifiable information.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.DEFAULT_PII_TERMS"},{"category":"constant","location":"#PackageScanner.LLM_PROVIDER_PATTERNS","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"LLM_PROVIDER_PATTERNS\n\nPatterns for proprietary/hosted LLM API usage across Python, R, and Stata: SDK imports/constructors and direct REST calls to each provider's hostname.\n\nDeliberately excludes purely local/open-weight model loading (e.g. AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained, local ollama calls) – consistent with the reproducibility literature's own recommendation that local execution is the more-reproducible path. This is a scoping choice, not a technical limitation: none of these patterns match transformers/ollama usage, so nothing needs to be explicitly filtered out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.LLM_PROVIDER_PATTERNS"},{"category":"constant","location":"#PackageScanner.README_REQUIRED_SECTIONS","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"README_REQUIRED_SECTIONS\n\nRequired sections for a replication package README, each mapped to a case-insensitive regex used to detect its presence by header or keyword proximity. Based on JPE-Reproducibility/PackageScanner.jl#7.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.README_REQUIRED_SECTIONS"},{"category":"constant","location":"#PackageScanner.README_URL_DOI_REGEX","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"README_URL_DOI_REGEX, README_ACCESS_DATE_REGEX\n\nPatterns used by readme_data_citations to flag whether a data source mention is accompanied by citation metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.README_URL_DOI_REGEX"},{"category":"constant","location":"#PackageScanner.SECRET_PATTERNS","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"SECRET_PATTERNS\n\nShared list of (secret_type, pattern) pairs for common API-key/credential formats. Single source of truth for both redact_secrets (strips a live credential out of text before it reaches a written report) and scan_secrets (flags that a credential is present, without ever capturing the matched text).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.SECRET_PATTERNS"},{"category":"type","location":"#PackageScanner.CodeQualityFinding","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"CodeQualityFinding\n\nA single finding from the code quality scanner.\n\nFields\n\nrule: rule identifier (e.g. \"missing_seed\", \"absolute_path\")\nrisk_level: :critical or :advisory\nlanguage: detected language (\"stata\", \"r\", \"python\", \"julia\", \"all\")\nfilepath: path to the file containing the finding (empty for package-level findings)\nline_number: line number of the finding, or nothing for package-level findings\nmessage: human-readable explanation for the report\ncontext: matched line or brief summary\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.CodeQualityFinding"},{"category":"type","location":"#PackageScanner.LLMUsageFinding","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"LLMUsageFinding\n\nA single detection of code that calls a proprietary/hosted LLM API.\n\nUnlike SecretFinding, context here is safe to show verbatim: matching an import openai line is not a secret.\n\nFields\n\nfilepath: path to the file containing the finding\nline_number: line number of the finding\nprovider: the LLM provider/service matched (e.g. \"OpenAI\", \"AWS Bedrock\")\ncontext: the matched line, stripped of leading/trailing whitespace\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.LLMUsageFinding"},{"category":"type","location":"#PackageScanner.PIIMatch","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"PIIMatch\n\nA structure representing a detected PII match.\n\nFields\n\nfilepath::String: Path to the file containing the match\nvariable_name::String: Name of the variable or line identifier\nvariable_label::Union{String, Nothing}: Variable label (if available)\nmatched_terms::Vector{String}: PII terms that matched\nsample_values::Vector{String}: Sample values from the data\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.PIIMatch"},{"category":"type","location":"#PackageScanner.SecretFinding","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"SecretFinding\n\nA single possible-secret detection.\n\nDeliberately carries no field for the matched text itself — only enough to locate (filepath, line_number) and classify (secret_type) the finding. This is a stronger guarantee than redacting at write time: there is no code path by which a live credential could ever reach a report, because there is no field to carry it in.\n\nFields\n\nfilepath: path to the file containing the finding\nline_number: line number of the finding\nsecret_type: human-readable label for the matched pattern (e.g. \"AWS access key ID\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.SecretFinding"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.check_file_paths-Tuple{Any}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"check_file_paths(filepath)\n\nRead each line of code and analyze. Looking for file paths and hardcoded numeric constants. Returns: (lines, classification, has_drive, hardcodes)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.check_file_paths"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.classify_files-Tuple{String, String, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"classify_files(pkg_path::String, kind::String, fp::String; relpath = false, pre_manifest=nothing)\n\nCount how many files belong to each of code, data, documentation according to their file ending.\n\nArguments\n\npkg_path: path to (extracted) replication package\nkind: what kind of classification is desired: code, data, docs\nfp: output folder path\npre_manifest: optional manifest with complete file list and extraction status\n\nOutputs txt files into folder fp which will be created if needed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.classify_files"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.create_example_package-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"create_example_package(at_root::String)\n\nCreate an example test package structure for testing purposes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.create_example_package"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.create_manifest_from_directory-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"create_manifest_from_directory(dir_path::String; size_threshold_gb=nothing, interactive=true)\n\nCreate a complete manifest of all files in a directory and mark which should be checked based on size. First unzips any nested .zip files found in the directory to ensure complete content scanning.\n\nArguments\n\ndir_path::String: Path to directory\nsize_threshold_gb::Union{Nothing,Float64}: Size threshold in GB (default: prompt user or 2.0)\ninteractive::Bool: Whether to prompt user for threshold (default: true)\n\nReturns\n\nDataFrame: Manifest with all files and check status\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.create_manifest_from_directory"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.create_manifest_from_zip-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"create_manifest_from_zip(zip_path::String; size_threshold_gb=nothing, interactive=true)\n\nCreate a complete manifest of all files in a zip archive and extract only files below size threshold.\n\nArguments\n\nzip_path::String: Path to zip file\nsize_threshold_gb::Union{Nothing,Float64}: Size threshold in GB (default: prompt user or 2.0)\ninteractive::Bool: Whether to prompt user for threshold (default: true)\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{DataFrame, String}: (manifest, extraction_directory)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.create_manifest_from_zip"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.data_dependency_audit-Tuple{Array, Array, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"data_dependency_audit(datafiles::Array, codefiles::Array, fp::String; min_stem_length = 3)\n\nFor each file in datafiles, search every file in codefiles for a word-bounded occurrence of that data file's basename or stem. Writes report-data-dependencies.md into fp.\n\nArguments\n\ndatafiles: paths to data files, as returned by classify_files(pkg_loc, \"data\", fp)\ncodefiles: paths to code files, as returned by classify_files(pkg_loc, \"code\", fp)\nfp: output folder path (must already exist, i.e. classify_files ran first)\nmin_stem_length: minimum stem length to use as a search token (default 3)\n\nReturns\n\nDict{String, Vector{String}} mapping each data file to the list of code files that reference it. An empty vector means the data file is an orphan: present in the package but no code reference to it was found.\n\nKnown limitations\n\nStatic string matching cannot catch filenames built dynamically at runtime (e.g. paste0(dir, year, \".csv\"), glob loops, file.path(...)), nor files fetched from a URL rather than loaded from a local path already in the package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.data_dependency_audit"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.data_file_tokens-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"data_file_tokens(datafile::AbstractString; min_stem_length = 3)\n\nCandidate search tokens for a data file: its basename (with extension) always, and its filename stem (without extension) only if at least min_stem_length characters long, to avoid false positives from very short stems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.data_file_tokens"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.detect_path_kinds-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"detect_path_kinds(line::String)\n\nDetects all relevant path patterns in a line of code. Returns tuple: (windows, unix, drive_letter)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.detect_path_kinds"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.extract_latex_text-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"extract_latex_text(paper_dir::String; main_files::Union{Nothing,Vector{String}} = nothing)\n\nFind candidate main .tex file(s) under paper_dir (see find_tex_roots), flatten each by inlining \\input/\\include, and concatenate.\n\nIf main_files is given explicitly, auto-discovery and the ambiguity check below are skipped entirely – exactly those files are flattened. Use this once a human has disambiguated (see below).\n\nIf main_files is omitted, find_tex_roots runs auto-discovery:\n\n0 candidates: returns text = \"\", roots = [], ambiguous = false. This is the \"no tex available\" signal – callers should fall back to the PDF (out of scope here).\n1 candidate: proceeds normally, flattens it.\n>1 candidates: this is genuinely ambiguous – a paper legitimately shipping main.tex + appendix.tex looks identical, structurally, to two unrelated conflicting drafts. Rather than guess (auto-concatenating or picking one), this returns text = \"\", ambiguous = true, and roots set to the full candidate list. The caller must surface this list and ask the user which file(s) are the actual paper source, then re-call with main_files set to the confirmed list.\n\nReturns a named tuple (text, roots, unresolved, ambiguous):\n\ntext: the flattened content of the resolved root file(s), concatenated. Empty when nothing was found, or when discovery was ambiguous and main_files was not given.\nroots: the main .tex files used (or, when ambiguous, all candidates found, for the caller to present as choices).\nunresolved: (command, arg, attempted_path) tuples for includes that could not be resolved (e.g. a macro that was never defined, or a typo).\nambiguous: true iff auto-discovery found more than one candidate and main_files was not given to disambiguate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.extract_latex_text"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.extract_metadata-Tuple{Any}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"extract_metadata(data)\n\nExtract metadata from a dataset, including variable names, labels, and sample values. Works with DataFrames, Dicts, and other tabular objects.\n\nArguments\n\ndata: A dataset (DataFrame, Dict, CSV.File, etc.)\nlabels: Optional variable labels\n\nReturns\n\nDict with keys: var_names, var_labels, samples\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.extract_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.file_paths-Tuple{Array, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"file_paths(files::Array, fp::String)\n\nTakes an array of file paths, reads each associated file and checks its content for  the existence of file paths of various kinds: windows C:\\file\\paths\\like\\this  or unix /paths/like/that. Also searches for numeric constants which could be hardcoded results.\n\nOutputs three markdown files into fp with partial reports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.file_paths"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.find_macro_defs_in_line-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"find_macro_defs_in_line(line::String)\n\nFind \\newcommand{\\x}{val}, \\newcommand*{\\x}{val}, \\renewcommand{\\x}{val}, and \\def\\x{val} definitions on a single (comment-stripped) line.\n\nReturns a Vector{Pair{String,String}} of (macro_name, value), values only matched when they contain no nested braces (simple literal macros, the common case for path-fragment macros like \\inputpath).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.find_macro_defs_in_line"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.find_pii_terms-Tuple{String, Vector{String}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"find_pii_terms(text::String, search_terms::Vector{String}; strict::Bool=false)\n\nSearch for PII terms in text string.\n\nArguments\n\ntext::String: Text to search\nsearch_terms::Vector{String}: List of PII terms to search for\nstrict::Bool=false: If true, use word boundary matching\n\nReturns\n\nVector{String}: List of matched terms\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.find_pii_terms"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.find_tex_roots-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"find_tex_roots(dir::String)\n\nRecursively search dir for candidate main .tex files: those containing \\begin{document} (so sub-included table/figure/preamble files are excluded), and excluding latexdiff artifacts (filename containing -diff or _diff).\n\nReturns a sorted Vector{String} of absolute paths. A paper submitted as main.tex + appendix.tex yields both; the caller concatenates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.find_tex_roots"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.findfile-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"findfile(directory, file; casesensitive = true)\n\nFind one specific file in the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.findfile"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.generate_code_quality_detail-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.CodeQualityFinding}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"generate_code_quality_detail(findings::Vector{CodeQualityFinding}) -> String\n\nGenerate the full, per-finding markdown listing of code quality findings, grouped by language, then by risk level (:critical first). This is the detailed body shared by the main report (when small) and the appendix (when the finding count is large).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.generate_code_quality_detail"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.generate_code_quality_summary_table-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.CodeQualityFinding}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"generate_code_quality_summary_table(findings::Vector{CodeQualityFinding}) -> String\n\nGenerate a compact per-language summary table (one row per language) of critical/advisory finding counts. Unlike the full per-finding listing, this table stays small regardless of how many findings there are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.generate_code_quality_summary_table"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.generate_detailed_appendix-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.PIIMatch}, Vector{PackageScanner.PIIMatch}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"generate_detailed_appendix(data_results::Vector{PIIMatch}, code_results::Vector{PIIMatch}; splitat=nothing)\n\nGenerate detailed markdown listing of all PII detections.\n\nArguments\n\ndata_results::Vector{PIIMatch}: Data file PII matches\ncode_results::Vector{PIIMatch}: Code file PII matches\nsplitat::Union{String,Nothing}=nothing: Optional string to split paths at\n\nReturns\n\nString: Markdown formatted detailed listing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.generate_detailed_appendix"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.generate_file_sizes_md5-Tuple{String, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"generate_file_sizes_md5(folder_path::String, output_path::String; large_size = 100, pre_manifest=nothing)\n\nRead entire package content and tabulate file sizes with file hash to check for duplicates.\n\nArguments\n\nfolder_path: Path to scan\noutput_path: Where to write reports\nlarge_size: Threshold in MB for \"large\" files (default: 100)\npre_manifest: Optional pre-generated manifest from zip inspection (includes non-extracted files)\n\nReturns a DataFrame with file metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.generate_file_sizes_md5"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.generate_summary_table-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.PIIMatch}, Vector{PackageScanner.PIIMatch}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"generate_summary_table(data_results::Vector{PIIMatch}, code_results::Vector{PIIMatch}; num_lines=20)\n\nGenerate a markdown summary table of PII detections.\n\nArguments\n\ndata_results::Vector{PIIMatch}: Data file PII matches\ncode_results::Vector{PIIMatch}: Code file PII matches\nnum_lines::Int=20: Maximum number of data rows in the short table\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{String,String}: (short_table, full_table) where short_table contains the first num_lines data rows plus headers, and full_table contains all rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.generate_summary_table"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.getPDFText-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"getPDFText(src, out) -> Dict\n\nExtract text from a PDF file.\n\nArguments\n\nsrc: Input PDF file path from where text is to be extracted\nout: Output TXT file path where the output will be written\n\nReturns a dictionary containing metadata of the document.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.getPDFText"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.hardcode_regex-Tuple{}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"hardcode_regex()\n\nRegular expression for detecting hardcoded numeric constants.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.hardcode_regex"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.inline_includes_in_line-Tuple{AbstractString, String, Dict{String, String}, Set{String}, Vector{Tuple{String, String, String}}, Int64, Int64}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"inline_includes_in_line(line, root_dir, macros, seen, unresolved, depth, max_depth)\n\nReplace every \\input{...}/\\include{...} on line with the recursively flattened content of its target (or a % UNRESOLVED INCLUDE placeholder).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.inline_includes_in_line"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.is_false_positive_context-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"is_false_positive_context(line::String)\n\nCheck if a line of code represents a false positive context.\n\nArguments\n\nline::String: Line of code to check\n\nReturns\n\nBool: true if line is likely a false positive\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.is_false_positive_context"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.is_unix_filepath-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"is_unix_filepath(line::String)\n\nFind a Unix filepath, omitting various comment and command strings. Looks for patterns like: here/we/have/a_/1234/unix-1/path\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.is_unix_filepath"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.is_windows_filepath-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"is_windows_filepath(line::String)\n\nFind a Windows filepath, omitting various comment and command strings. Looks for patterns like: this\\is\\123\\a\\path-yes\\1t\\is\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.is_windows_filepath"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.is_word_char-Tuple{Char}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"is_word_char(c::Char)\n\nA character that can be part of an identifier/filename token (letters, digits, underscore). Used to check word-boundaries around a matched filename token, so that e.g. a short stem like \"data\" does not match inside an unrelated longer word.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.is_word_char"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.language_of-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"language_of(filepath::String) -> Union{String,Nothing}\n\nMap a file path to a language key used in the config (\"stata\", \"r\", \"python\", \"julia\"). Returns nothing for unrecognised extensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.language_of"},{"category":"function","location":"#PackageScanner.load_data_metadata","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"load_data_metadata(filepath::String, max_rows = 1000)\n\nLoad metadata from a data file. For CSV files, uses Julia's CSV package. For other file types, uses R's rio package. Returns a dictionary with variable names, labels, and sample values, or nothing if the file cannot be loaded.\n\nArguments\n\nfilepath::String: Path to the data file\nmax_rows::Int: Maximum number of rows to read (default: 1000)\n\nReturns\n\nDict with keys: var_names, var_labels, samples\nnothing if file cannot be loaded\n\nExamples\n\nmetadata = load_data_metadata(\"data/survey.dta\")\nprintln(metadata[\"var_names\"])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.load_data_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.mycp-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"bug workaround until https://github.com/JuliaLang/julia/pull/59662\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.mycp"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.path_splitter-Tuple{String, Any}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"path_splitter(path::String, at)\n\nSplit a path at a specific delimiter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.path_splitter"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.precheck_package-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"precheck_package(pkg_loc::String; pre_manifest=nothing)\n\nComprehensive check of a replication package in directory pkg_loc.\n\nIt is assumed that pkg_loc is descendant in a directory structure of this kind,  i.e. what is implemented in https://github.com/JPE-Reproducibility/JPEtemplate\n\nIn this example, pkg_loc = abspath(replication-package):\n\n.\n├── generated\n├── images\n├── LICENSE\n├── replication-package\n├── package-output-map.xlsx\n├── README.md\n└── TEMPLATE.qmd\n\nArguments\n\npkg_loc: Path to the replication package directory\npre_manifest: Optional DataFrame containing pre-generated manifest (for selective extraction)\n\nPerforms\n\nFile classification (code/data/docs)\nFile metadata analysis (sizes, checksums, duplicates)\nCode line counting (via cloc)\nFile path analysis in code files\nPII detection in data and code files\nREADME analysis\n\nAll reports are written to generated/ folder at the package root.\n\nExamples\n\n# Regular workflow (extract and check everything)\nprecheck_package(\"replication-package\")\n\n# Large package workflow (selective extraction)\npkg_dir, manifest = prepare_package_for_precheck(\"large.zip\")\nprecheck_package(pkg_dir, pre_manifest=manifest)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.precheck_package"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.prepare_package_for_precheck-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"prepare_package_for_precheck(input_path::String; kwargs...)\n\nUnified function that handles both zip files and directories. Creates manifest and applies size filtering in both cases.\n\nArguments\n\ninput_path::String: Path to zip file or directory\nsize_threshold_gb::Union{Nothing,Float64}: Size threshold in GB (default: prompt or 2.0)\ninteractive::Bool: Whether to prompt user (default: true)\n\nReturns\n\nTuple{String, DataFrame}: (package_directory, manifest)\n\nExamples\n\n# Zip file with interactive prompt\npkg_dir, manifest = prepare_package_for_precheck(\"large.zip\")\n\n# Directory with fixed threshold\npkg_dir, manifest = prepare_package_for_precheck(\"/path/to/pkg\", \n                                                  size_threshold_gb=1.5, \n                                                  interactive=false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.prepare_package_for_precheck"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.rdir-Tuple{AbstractString, Glob.FilenameMatch}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"rdir(dir::AbstractString, pat::Glob.FilenameMatch)\n\nRecursively search a directory tree for files matching the pattern.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.rdir"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.read_README-Tuple{Any, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"read_README(pkg_loc, fp::String)\n\nFind the README in the package and read from either .md or .pdf format.  Then produce a report with mentions of interesting terms like software used,  whether confidential, etc.\n\nArguments\n\npkg_loc: Package location\nfp: Output folder path\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.read_README"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.read_and_unzip_directory-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"read_and_unzip_directory(dir_path::String)\n\nRead contents of a directory and unzip any .zip files using system unzip command. Extracts zip files to the same directory where they reside.\n\nArguments\n\ndir_path::String: Path to the directory to read\n\nReturns\n\nVector{String}: All file paths in the directory (after unzipping)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.read_and_unzip_directory"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.readme_data_citations-Tuple{AbstractString, Array}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"readme_data_citations(readme_text::AbstractString, datafiles::Array; window = 2, min_stem_length = 3)\n\nFor each file in datafiles, check whether the README mentions it (by basename or stem, reusing data_file_tokens/token_occurs from the data dependency audit), and if so whether a URL/DOI and an access date are present within window lines of the first mention.\n\nReturns a Vector{NamedTuple} with fields datafile, mentioned, has_url_or_doi, has_access_date — one row per data file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.readme_data_citations"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.readme_section_checklist-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"readme_section_checklist(readme_text::AbstractString)\n\nFor each entry of README_REQUIRED_SECTIONS, find the first matching line.\n\nReturns a Vector{Tuple{String, Union{Int,Nothing}}} of (section_name, line_number) pairs, in the same order as README_REQUIRED_SECTIONS; line_number is nothing if the section was not found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.readme_section_checklist"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.readme_structure_audit-Tuple{String, Array, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"readme_structure_audit(pkg_loc::String, datafiles::Array, fp::String)\n\nFind the README in fp's sibling documentation-files.txt manifest (as written by classify_files(pkg_loc, \"docs\", fp)), run the section checklist and data-citation audit against it, and write report-readme-structure.md into fp.\n\nOnly handles .md READMEs directly (PDF extraction is left to read_README's existing getPDFText fallback, out of scope here per JPE-Reproducibility/JPE.jl#29).\n\nReturns nothing if no markdown README was found (still writes a report noting this).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.readme_structure_audit"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.redact_secrets-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"redact_secrets(s::AbstractString) -> String\n\nReplace substrings matching common API-key/credential patterns (SECRET_PATTERNS, defined in constants.jl) with a placeholder. Applied to any raw file content (code context, data samples) before it is embedded in a written report, so a live credential found in a scanned package never round-trips into our own generated (and git-tracked) output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.redact_secrets"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.resolve_include_path-Tuple{AbstractString, String, Dict{String, String}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"resolve_include_path(arg::String, root_dir::String, macros::Dict{String,String})\n\nSubstitute macros in arg, normalize it against root_dir, and append .tex if no extension was given and the bare path doesn't itself exist. Returns the resolved absolute path (which may not exist – check with isfile).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.resolve_include_path"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.resolve_includes!-Tuple{String, String, Dict{String, String}, Set{String}, Vector{Tuple{String, String, String}}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"resolve_includes!(filepath, root_dir, macros, seen, unresolved; depth=0, max_depth=10)\n\nRead filepath, strip comments line by line, collect macro definitions as they're encountered (document order, matching how LaTeX itself resolves macros), and recursively inline every \\input{...}/\\include{...} target. \\includegraphics{...} is left untouched (it's a pointer to a binary figure file, not inlineable text, and token_occurs from the data dependency audit can cross-reference these paths against package output files directly).\n\nAll include paths resolve relative to root_dir (the paper's root), not to the including file's own directory – matching how \\inputpath-style macros are used in practice (defined once relative to the root).\n\nseen is the set of absolute paths on the current inclusion chain (cycle guard); unresolved accumulates (command, arg, attempted_path) tuples for includes that couldn't be resolved, mutated in place.\n\nReturns the flattened text of filepath with all includes inlined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.resolve_includes!"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.scan_code_file-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"scan_code_file(filepath::String; strict::Bool=false, custom_terms::Vector{String}=String[])\n\nScan a code file for PII term references.\n\nArguments\n\nfilepath::String: Path to code file\nstrict::Bool=false: Use strict word boundary matching\ncustom_terms::Vector{String}=String[]: Additional PII terms to search for\n\nReturns\n\nVector{PIIMatch}: List of detected PII references\n\nExamples\n\nmatches = scan_code_file(\"src/analysis.R\")\nmatches = scan_code_file(\"src/analysis.R\", strict=true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.scan_code_file"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.scan_code_files-Tuple{Vector{String}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"scan_code_files(code_files::Vector{String}; strict::Bool=false, custom_terms::Vector{String}=String[])\n\nScan multiple code files for PII references.\n\nArguments\n\ncode_files::Vector{String}: Vector of code file paths\nstrict::Bool=false: Use strict word boundary matching\ncustom_terms::Vector{String}=String[]: Additional PII terms\n\nReturns\n\nVector{PIIMatch}: All detected PII references across files\n\nExamples\n\ncode_files = [\"src/clean.R\", \"src/analysis.py\"]\nmatches = scan_code_files(code_files)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.scan_code_files"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.scan_code_quality-Tuple{Vector{String}, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"scan_code_quality(codefiles::Vector{String}, config_path::String) -> Vector{CodeQualityFinding}\n\nScan codefiles for high-risk coding patterns defined in config_path (TOML).\n\nReturns a vector of CodeQualityFinding items, each carrying a risk_level (:critical or :advisory), the affected file and line, and a human-readable message.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.scan_code_quality"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.scan_data_file-Tuple{String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"scan_data_file(filepath::String; strict::Bool=false, custom_terms::Vector{String}=String[])\n\nScan a data file for PII indicators in variable names and labels.\n\nArguments\n\nfilepath::String: Path to data file\nstrict::Bool=false: Use strict word boundary matching\ncustom_terms::Vector{String}=String[]: Additional PII terms to search for\n\nReturns\n\nVector{PIIMatch}: List of detected PII matches\n\nExamples\n\nmatches = scan_data_file(\"data/survey.dta\")\nmatches = scan_data_file(\"data/survey.dta\", strict=true)\nmatches = scan_data_file(\"data/survey.dta\", custom_terms=[\"patient_id\"])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.scan_data_file"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.scan_data_files-Tuple{Vector{String}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"scan_data_files(data_files::Vector{String}; strict::Bool=false, custom_terms::Vector{String}=String[])\n\nScan multiple data files for PII indicators.\n\nArguments\n\ndata_files::Vector{String}: Vector of data file paths\nstrict::Bool=false: Use strict word boundary matching\ncustom_terms::Vector{String}=String[]: Additional PII terms\n\nReturns\n\nVector{PIIMatch}: All detected PII matches across files\n\nExamples\n\ndata_files = [\"data/survey.dta\", \"data/admin.csv\"]\nmatches = scan_data_files(data_files)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.scan_data_files"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.scan_llm_usage-Tuple{Vector{String}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"scan_llm_usage(codefiles::Vector{String}) -> Vector{LLMUsageFinding}\n\nScan codefiles line-by-line for calls to proprietary/hosted LLM APIs (LLM_PROVIDER_PATTERNS). Stops at the first matching provider per line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.scan_llm_usage"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.scan_secrets-Tuple{Vector{String}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"scan_secrets(codefiles::Vector{String}) -> Vector{SecretFinding}\n\nScan codefiles line-by-line for common API-key/credential formats (SECRET_PATTERNS, shared with redact_secrets in reporting.jl). Stops at the first matching pattern per line — a line worth alerting on doesn't need a second match counted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.scan_secrets"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.strip_latex_comment-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"strip_latex_comment(line::String)\n\nRemove everything from the first unescaped % onward (i.e. not preceded by a literal \\). Does not account for verbatim environments, where % is not a comment marker – a known limitation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.strip_latex_comment"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.substitute_macros-Tuple{AbstractString, Dict{String, String}}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"substitute_macros(s::String, macros::Dict{String,String})\n\nReplace every \\name token in s with macros[\"name\"] if known, iterating a few times to handle a macro expanding to another macro. Unknown tokens are left untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.substitute_macros"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.token_occurs-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"token_occurs(token::AbstractString, text::AbstractString)\n\nCheck whether token occurs in text as a whole, word-bounded match (not merely as a substring of a longer token). Dots and other non-word characters in token (as in a filename like survey.dta) are matched literally.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.token_occurs"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.write_code_quality_report-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.CodeQualityFinding}, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"write_code_quality_report(findings::Vector{CodeQualityFinding}, output_dir::String;\n                           report_file=\"report-code-quality.md\",\n                           appendix_file=\"report-code-quality-appendix.md\",\n                           max_inline::Int=40)\n\nWrite the code-quality report. Mirrors write_pii_report's main+appendix split: if there are more than max_inline findings, report_file gets only a per-language summary table plus a link to appendix_file, which carries the full per-finding listing. Below that threshold, the full listing is written inline and appendix_file is written empty — it is always created (even if empty) so that a Quarto {{< include >}} of it never errors on a missing file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.write_code_quality_report"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.write_extraction_summary-Tuple{DataFrames.DataFrame, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"write_extraction_summary(manifest::DataFrame, output_path::String)\n\nWrite a summary report about which files were extracted/checked vs catalogued only.\n\nArguments\n\nmanifest::DataFrame: Manifest with extraction and check status\noutput_path::String: Directory to write report to\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.write_extraction_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.write_illegal_files_report-Tuple{DataFrames.DataFrame, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"write_illegal_files_report(manifest::DataFrame, output_path::String)\n\nGenerate a report of files that are not valid regular files (broken symlinks, special files, etc.)\n\nArguments\n\nmanifest::DataFrame: Manifest with isvalidfile column\noutput_path::String: Directory to write report to\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.write_illegal_files_report"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.write_llm_usage_report-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.LLMUsageFinding}, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"write_llm_usage_report(findings::Vector{LLMUsageFinding}, output_dir::String; report_file=\"report-llm-reproducibility.md\")\n\nWrite the LLM-API-usage report: a per-provider list of findings (if any), followed by a note on why LLM outputs are not exactly reproducible even at temperature=0, grounded in Coqueret, Llull, Oswald, Pérignon, Scheuch & Vilhuber, \"Randomness in large language models: What researchers need to know (and report)\" (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.24372).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.write_llm_usage_report"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.write_pii_report-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.PIIMatch}, Vector{PackageScanner.PIIMatch}, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"write_pii_report(data_results, code_results, output_dir; kwargs...)\n\nWrite PII detection reports (main summary and detailed appendix).\n\nArguments\n\ndata_results::Vector{PIIMatch}: Data file PII matches\ncode_results::Vector{PIIMatch}: Code file PII matches\noutput_dir::String: Directory to write reports to\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nsplitat::Union{String,Nothing}=nothing: String to split file paths at\nmain_file::String=\"report-pii.md\": Name of main report file\nappendix_file::String=\"report-pii-appendix.md\": Name of appendix file\n\nExamples\n\nwrite_pii_report(data_results, code_results, \"/output\", splitat=\"/project/\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.write_pii_report"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.write_pii_report_simple-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.PIIMatch}, Vector{PackageScanner.PIIMatch}, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"write_pii_report_simple(data_results, code_results, fp; splitat=nothing)\n\nWrite single PII report in simple bullet-list format.\n\nArguments\n\ndata_results::Vector{PIIMatch}: Data file PII matches\ncode_results::Vector{PIIMatch}: Code file PII matches  \nfp::String: Directory to write report to\nsplitat::Union{String,Nothing}=nothing: String to split file paths at\n\nExamples\n\nwrite_pii_report_simple(data_results, code_results, \"/output\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.write_pii_report_simple"},{"category":"method","location":"#PackageScanner.write_secrets_report-Tuple{Vector{PackageScanner.SecretFinding}, String}","page":"PackageScanner.jl","text":"write_secrets_report(findings::Vector{SecretFinding}, output_dir::String; report_file=\"report-secrets.md\")\n\nWrite the secret-scan report. findings carries only filepath, line_number, and secret_type — never the matched text itself — so there is no way for a live credential to end up in this report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PackageScanner.write_secrets_report"}]
}
